{"body": "I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. The documented rule says: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text, because a model places its final answer last and marks it: a reply that quotes its previous answer and then gives a corrected one is scored on the correction.\" Reply A is the string \"```text\\n{\\\"a\\\": 1}\\n```\\n{\\\"b\\\": 2}\". Reply B is the string \"{\\\"b\\\": 2}\". For Reply A, the rule requires the answer {\"a\": 1}, because there is a code fence holding an object, and the last object inside a code fence is {\"a\": 1}. For Reply B, the rule requires the answer {\"b\": 2}, because there is no code fence, so the last top-level object is {\"b\": 2}. The check gives the same answer for both: it returns {\"b\": 2} for each, because its fence regex only matches ```json, ```JSON, or bare ```, so it misses the ```text fence and falls back to the last top-level object. This conjecture would be false if the check returned {\"a\": 1} for Reply A and {\"b\": 2} for Reply B, or if the documented rule were read as excluding ```text fences."}